188
00:13:59,943 --> 00:14:05,951
Predict before the failure scenario: of
hooks/, info/, description, config, refs/,

189
00:14:05,991 --> 00:14:11,999
objects/ and HEAD, which can be taken away
while git status still works, and which

190
00:14:12,039 --> 00:14:16,351
can't? Write your list first.
Then run the lab and compare.

191
00:14:18,191 --> 00:14:23,282
Q14: "What makes a directory a Git repository?
Which files in a fresh .git can you

192
00:14:23,322 --> 00:14:25,034
delete without breaking it?"

193
00:14:25,374 --> 00:14:29,602
Answer out loud, after you've done the
lab. A strong answer is based on the

194
00:14:29,642 --> 00:14:34,382
experiment and not on a guess. It names what
Git looks for when it decides whether a

195
00:14:34,422 --> 00:14:39,162
directory is a repository, separates what
came from a template from what Git depends

196
00:14:39,202 --> 00:14:42,064
on, and says how you would
restore what is missing.

197
00:14:43,904 --> 00:14:48,820
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: git init creates a .git directory

198
00:14:48,860 --> 00:14:53,163
with a HEAD that names an unborn
branch, and nothing else of substance.

199
00:14:53,203 --> 00:14:58,179
Editing a file changes nothing under .git.
git add writes the content as an object

200
00:14:58,219 --> 00:15:03,135
and writes the index. git commit writes
tree and commit objects, creates or moves

201
00:15:03,175 --> 00:15:05,765
the branch ref, and
appends to the reflogs.

202
00:15:06,105 --> 00:15:10,977
HEAD names a branch and the branch names a
commit, so a commit moves the branch and

203
00:15:11,017 --> 00:15:12,219
HEAD stays as it was.

